# Life Admin — Deployment Guide

## 1. Deployment Target

Recommended:
- Next.js application: Vercel.
- PostgreSQL: managed PostgreSQL provider compatible with Prisma.

Any equivalent production hosting platform may be used if it supports Next.js correctly.

## 2. Required Production Variables

Example:

```env
DATABASE_URL="production-postgresql-connection-string"
AUTH_SECRET="strong-random-secret"
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="https://your-domain.example"
```

Add provider-specific authentication variables if the chosen auth implementation requires them.

Never commit the real `.env` file.

## 3. Git Preparation

Check:

```bash
git status
```

Make sure secrets are not staged.

Recommended `.gitignore` entries include:

```text
.env
.env.local
.env.production
node_modules
.next
```

Commit:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "feat: complete life admin application"
git push origin main
```

## 4. Production Database

Create a production PostgreSQL database.

Obtain:

```text
DATABASE_URL
```

Test the connection from the deployment environment.

## 5. Prisma Production Migration

Do not use development migration commands against production.

Use:

```bash
npx prisma migrate deploy
```

Then ensure the application uses the generated Prisma client.

## 6. Build

Typical build command:

```bash
npm run build
```

Start command for a traditional Node host:

```bash
npm run start
```

For Vercel, the platform detects and builds the Next.js application.

## 7. Vercel Deployment

Steps:

1. Push repository to GitHub.
2. Create/import the project in Vercel.
3. Select the GitHub repository.
4. Configure production environment variables.
5. Deploy.
6. Check build logs.
7. Open the production URL.

## 8. Production Smoke Test

Test:

### Authentication
- Register.
- Login.
- Logout.
- Protected route access.

### Dashboard
- Correct document count.
- Correct expiring-soon count.
- Correct expired count.
- Upcoming renewals display.

### CRUD
- Create.
- Read.
- Update.
- Delete.

### Security
- User A cannot access User B's document.
- Unauthenticated user cannot access private API.

### Search/filter
- Search title.
- Search document number.
- Category filter.
- Status filter.
- Sorting.

### Responsive UI
- Mobile.
- Tablet.
- Desktop.

## 9. Deployment Safety

Before production:
- Do not expose database URL.
- Do not expose auth secret.
- Do not log credentials.
- Verify CORS/security configuration if external clients are introduced.
- Verify production cookies/session settings.
- Verify error messages do not reveal internals.

## 10. Database Backups

Use the selected PostgreSQL provider's backup capabilities.

The application should not assume that source code backups are database backups.

## 11. CI/CD

A basic GitHub workflow should eventually:

1. Install dependencies.
2. Run lint/type checks.
3. Run tests.
4. Build the application.

Example conceptual pipeline:

```text
Push / Pull Request
        |
        v
Install
        |
        v
Lint + Type Check
        |
        v
Tests
        |
        v
Build
        |
        v
Deploy
```

Do not deploy known failing builds.

## 12. Final Submission Checklist

- [ ] GitHub repository available.
- [ ] Live deployment available.
- [ ] README contains setup instructions.
- [ ] Environment variables documented without exposing secrets.
- [ ] Database migrations included.
- [ ] Core CRUD works.
- [ ] Authentication works.
- [ ] Ownership/security works.
- [ ] Validation works.
- [ ] Search/filter works.
- [ ] Dashboard works.
- [ ] Responsive UI works.
- [ ] Tests included and passing.
- [ ] Optional AI clearly separated from core functionality.
- [ ] Required author/project footer links included.

## 13. README Minimum Content

README should contain:
- Project overview.
- Features.
- Technology stack.
- Architecture overview.
- Local setup.
- Environment variables.
- Database setup.
- Migration commands.
- Test commands.
- Build/deployment commands.
- Live URL.
- GitHub URL.
- Optional AI feature description.

## 14. Post-Deployment Monitoring

After deployment:
- Check application logs.
- Check failed API requests.
- Check database connectivity.
- Verify authentication cookies/session behavior.
- Verify production environment variables.
- Monitor build/deployment failures.

Fix production issues through Git commits and redeployments rather than editing production manually.
